206   C  TITL$RECOMMENDED NUCLEAR STRUCTURE AND DECAY DATA FOR A=206 ISOBARS    
206   c  AUTH$F.G. KONDEV                                                       
206   c  INST$Physics Division                                                  
206  #c       Argonne National Laboratory                                       
206  #c       9700 South Cass Avenue                                            
206  #c       Lemont, Illinois 60439, USA                                       
206   C  CIT$NDS 201, 346 (2025)                                                
206   c  ABST$Evaluated nuclear structure and decay data for all nuclei with    
206  2c  mass number A=206 ({+206}Pt, {+206}Au, {+206}Hg, {+206}Tl, {+206}Pb,   
206  3c  {+206}Bi, {+206}Po, {+206}At, {+206}Rn, {+206}Fr, {+206}Ra and         
206  4c  {+206}Ac), are presented. All available experimental data are compiled 
206  5c  and evaluated, and best values for level and |g-ray energies, quantum  
206  6c  numbers, lifetimes, |g-ray intensities and transition probabilities,   
206  7c  as well as other nuclear properties, are recommended.  Inconsistencies 
206  8c  and discrepancies that exist in the literature are discussed. A number 
206  9c  of computer codes (https://www-nds.iaea.org/public/ensdf_pgm/)         
206  Bc  developed by members of the NSDD network were used during the          
206  Cc  evaluation process.  This work supersedes the earlier evaluation by    
206  Dc  F.G. Kondev (2008Ko21), published in {INuclear Data Sheets} {B109},    
206  Ec  1527 (2008).                                                           
206   c  CUT$Literature up to January 21, 2025 was consulted. The main source   
206  2c  used for bibliographic information is the NSR database (2014Pr09):     
206  3c  www.nndc.bnl.gov/nsr/.                                                 
206   c  ACKN$The author expresses his gratitude to Drs. E.A. McCutchan and C.  
206  2c  Morse from the National Nuclear Data Center (BNL) for their assistance 
206  3c  during the preparation of this manuscript for publication.  The author 
206  4c  is thankful to Dr. C. Morse for his critical reading of the manuscript 
206  5c  and many useful comments, and suggestions.                             
206   c  FUND$This work is supported by the Office of Nuclear Physics, Office   
206  xc  of Science, U.S. Department of Energy under contract                   
206  xc  DE-AC02-06CH11357.                                                     

