206HG    COULOMB EXCITATION            2023MO04                  25NDS    202503
206HG  H TYP=FUL$AUT=F.G. KONDEV$CIT=NDS 201, 346 (2025)$CUT=21-Jan-2025$       
206HG c  2023Mo04: {+206}Hg beam produced at the HIE-ISOLDE facility at CERN    
206HG2c  using a molten lead target bombarded with 1.4 GeV protons, with an     
206HG3c  intensity of |J 0.6 |mA. The produced mercury isotopes were laser      
206HG4c  ionized, mass separated using the General Purpose Separator (GPS), and 
206HG5c  charge bred in an electron-beam ion source (REX-EBIS).                 
206HG6c  {+206}Hg{+46+} ions were post-accelerated using the HIE-ISOLDE linear  
206HG7c  accelerator, to an energy of 4.195 MeV/u, with a beam repetition rate  
206HG8c  of 300 ms. The typical beam intensity was |J 7.8|*10{+5} pps.          
206HG9c  The {+206}Hg beam impinged on a 2 mg/cm{+2}-thick target, made either  
206HGac  of {+94}Mo or {+104}Pd. Following Coulomb excitation, |g rays          
206HGbc  depopulating states in both the projectile and target nuclei were      
206HGcc  detected by the 23 HPGe detectors comprising the Miniball array, in    
206HGdc  coincidence with recoiling particles detected in an annular            
206HGec  Double-Sided Silicon Strip Detector (DSSSD). Both sides of the DSSSD   
206HGfc  array consisted of 4 quadrants, with the front of each divided into    
206HGgc  16 annular rings (strips), and the back into a further 24 sectors,     
206HGhc  coupled into 12 pairs when read out. This covered a scattering angle   
206HGic  range from 20|' to 59|'in the laboratory reference frame.              
206HGjc  Measured: E|g, I|g, recoil-|g coin.                                    
